{"author":{"id":"746a4d30afc20b78119cb8ed7c194f988174fc252f0e9d9eb92022244ef86db3"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1779507344,"nonce":"4pRPuFW/FQ/0hTpkgVphfJ4qpfA=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOK4fCvM8AAAABDJ7JHQ","github-url":"https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/167","origin":"github"},"title":"Comprehensive cross-deployment power-usage analysis (2026-05-19 → 21 → 22 single charge cycle)","message":"## Goal\n\nComprehensive characterization of BizzyBoat power usage across the **chained 2026-05-19 → 2026-05-21 → 2026-05-22 deployments** — all three ran on a single charge cycle (no recharge between any of them), with today's recovery + charger-connect closing out the cycle. This is a near-complete one-charge-cycle dataset on the Torqeedo Power 24-3500 pair under varied operational loads, plus a recharge curve from sub-storage-range.\n\nEnd-state deliverable: a power-usage report (markdown + plots) that gives the class instructor the planning numbers needed to schedule between-cohort battery swaps, set survey-pattern energy budgets, and predict run-time at varied SOC. Goes in `~/data/logs/analysis/` alongside `2026-05-22_deployment.db`.\n\n## Data inventory\n\n- **2026-05-22 bag DB**: `~/data/logs/analysis/2026-05-22_deployment.db` (already extracted via [`bag_to_sqlite`](https://github.com/rolker/marine_tools/blob/jazzy/bag_analysis/) on `~/data/logs/bizzyboat/2026-05-22T21-05-06+00-00`; 1.9 GB, 4.18 M messages, covers 17:05:07 → 20:34:23 EDT)\n- **2026-05-21 bag DB**: `~/data/logs/analysis/2026-05-21_deployment.db` (precedent from prior wrap-up; 1.3 GB)\n- **2026-05-19**: source bags in `~/data/logs/bizzyboat/`; bag-to-sqlite extraction needed if not already present\n- **gabby boot history**: [`bizzyboat_project11/docs/gabby_boot_history_2026-05-15_to_2026-05-22.md`](../blob/jazzy/bizzyboat_project11/docs/gabby_boot_history_2026-05-15_to_2026-05-22.md) — gives gabby boot timestamps for each deployment (ground-truth power-cycle bounds)\n- **Per-deployment dev logs** at `docs/logs/2026/2026-05-{19,21,22}_dev_logs.md` + `_gabby_logs.md` + `_salmon_logs.md` — operator observations, voltage snapshots, mission profiles, anomalies\n- **Mission events** to anchor analysis windows: launch / recovery times via `bag_analysis.launch_recovery`; mission state transitions via `/bizzy/marine/status/mission_manager`; mode transitions via `/bizzy/mavros/state`\n\n## Scope — four sub-analyses\n\n### 1. Cross-deployment battery model (3-deployment single charge cycle)\n\nBuild the discharge voltage curve across the chained 3-deployment cycle:\n\n- Stitch the three deployment bags into a continuous voltage-vs-cumulative-runtime series, with gaps for rest periods between sessions (rest-state voltage recovery is itself a data point — characterize it).\n- Identify and label the per-deployment milestones (launch, recovery, mode transitions, mission profile shifts).\n- Capture the charging response curve from today's post-recovery segment (charger-connect ~20:34 EDT through bag end at 20:34:23 EDT — but the bag may be too short; the charging continues beyond. If a follow-up bag was started, include it; otherwise note that the charging-curve sample window is short).\n- **Class-planning output**: projection of time-to-storage-voltage (22.6–24.2 V manufacturer range) at typical class operational profiles.\n\n### 2. Voltage-sag-vs-SOC characterization\n\nEffective internal-resistance proxy across SOC:\n\n- Identify sag events across all three deployments where voltage drops sharply under thrust transients then rebounds.\n- Pair each sag magnitude with the mean-SOC voltage at the time.\n- Build the sag-vs-SOC curve. Today gave us ~0.2 V sag at 22.8 V loaded (manual-mode drift test); yesterday observed ~1.7 V sag at higher SOC. Multiple samples across the cycle should yield a clear curve.\n- **Class-planning output**: voltage headroom estimate vs. LVD at different SOC for typical thrust-burst profiles (e.g., turn transients during a tight-spacing survey).\n\n### 3. Speed-power model from 1 / 2 / 4 kt anchors\n\nToday's deployment included explicit speed-anchor runs at 1, 2, and 4 kt (per the dev log \"Speed series executed\" entry). Plus survey-load datapoints at ~2 kt with turn transients.\n\n- For each anchor, extract `/bizzy/mavros/battery` (voltage), thrust commands, and forward-speed; compute mean and variance per anchor.\n- If current measurements are available from a non-placeholder source (note: `mavros/battery.current` reads ~0.01 A as a placeholder on this hull), use them; otherwise estimate power from voltage drop alone with a noted assumption about battery internal resistance from sub-analysis #2.\n- Fit a power-vs-speed curve. The 4 kt anchor was at low SOC — interesting data point at the upper-power / lower-headroom corner.\n- **Class-planning output**: energy budget per square meter of survey at different speeds (combine with the per-mission XTE if useful).\n\n### 4. Phase-by-phase draw breakdown\n\nPer-phase energy budget across today's deployment (and ideally across the other two for comparison):\n\n| Phase | Today's window | What's drawing |\n|---|---|---|\n| gabby idle dockside (boat on battery, ROS not yet recording) | 09:20 → 17:05 EDT | gabby + boat subsystems |\n| ROS bringup + Block 1 dockside | 17:05 → 17:28:52 EDT | + segmentation, costmap, full ROS stack |\n| In-water autonomous + manual mix | 17:28:52 → 20:12:18 EDT | + thrusters under load |\n| Recovery dockside, charger not yet connected | 20:12:18 → ~20:34 EDT | back to dockside subsystems |\n| Charging | ~20:34 → ongoing | charger replaces battery as source |\n\nFor each phase, derive the slope of the voltage curve (ΔV/Δt) as a proxy for average draw. Compare phase slopes to identify the dominant consumers and the marginal cost of e.g. \"leaving the boat on but not deployed\" vs \"running autonomy.\"\n\n- **Class-planning output**: budget for \"boat sitting at the pier with ROS up\" minutes vs \"active autonomy\" minutes. Drives the SOP for class operations (e.g., \"don't leave the boat on while waiting for the next cohort to arrive\").\n\n## Cross-references\n\n- [#162](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/162) Battery annunciator threshold logic — this analysis can confirm the threshold values that should fire and validate them against the FCU `BATT_LOW_VOLT` setting.\n- [#164](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/164), [#165](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/165) — bag analysis for path-follower undulation + hover drift may produce datapoints feeding the speed-power model.\n- [#160](https://github.com/rolker/unh_echoboats_project11/issues/160) — originating deployment.\n\n## Deliverable shape\n\nA markdown report at `~/data/logs/analysis/2026-05-22_power_analysis.md` (or similar name) plus supporting plots. PR-attachable summary for the class-prep roadmap; full report stays local in `~/data/logs/analysis/`.\n\nHard deadline: results useful for class-prep should land **before June 4** (dev freeze). Partial results \u003e nothing — the four sub-analyses are independently useful and can ship incrementally.\n\nPart of #160.\n\n---\n**Authored-By**: `Claude Code Agent`\n**Model**: `Claude Opus 4.7 (1M context)`","files":null}]}